   Mithrandir the Wizard is happily exploring Gehennom, and is lugging
around 78000 gold pieces.  Being rather stingy, he hates the idea of
just throwing them away...  but he knows he will not find another
peaceful priest to make a donation, so what could he use gold for?

   In this mood, I remembered that Moria has a Black Market, where you
can find many useful items at outrageous prices.  So I think:  why not
implementing a Black Market for NetHack 3.1?  Then gold can be useful
again for high-level characters, and the idea of robbing Fort Ludios
becomes more interesting!

   Here are my ideas; if you have suggestions, post or e-mail me.
I'll post a summary before I start coding the Black Market.

		Effects:

 1) It should be impossible to steal from the Black Market, or kill
    or charm the shopkeeper. 
 2) The Black Market should be packed with items of every kind,
    excluding Artifacts (or should we allow for the lesser Artifacts?).  
 3) Prices should be *really* outrageous; e.g.: 
    most items: 20*P;
    amulets, magic armor, magic tools: 50*P.
    [magic armor is e.g., cloak of magic resistance, speed boots;
     magic tools are e.g., magic lamp, horn of plenty;
     P = normal price.]
 4) The shopkeeper should have a huge amount of gold, so he can pay a
    reasonable price for your (identified) precious gems.

I think that high prices and the impossibility of robbing make the
Black Market useful without upsetting the balance of the game.

		 Implementation:

 1) The Black Market is a "side dungeon", � la Fort Ludios, therefore
    it is impossible to dig down. 
 2) Sorry, pets are not allowed in the premises.  Not-human customers
    are not welcome.  [Should invisible customers be welcome?  The
    shopkeeper sees invisible anyway...]
 3) A magic portal to the Black Market appears in every game, between
    the Quest portal and Medusa level.  You recognize it from
    advertisements (Something is engraved here on the floor.  You
    read: "visit Joe's grocery") and sounds (Someone says "food
    rations, only 900 zorkmids each").
 4) The shopkeeper is *very* high-level, and protected vs. everything.
    If you kill him, something horrible happens and you piss off your
    god.  Alternatively, if you hit the shopkeeper you are magically
    send back to the main dungeon and the portal disappears.
